| opencode                                                        | craze                                                                                                                                               | why |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----|
| ripgrep comes from opencode's own binary service                | rg found on PATH, once per session (grep and glob share the lookup); without it both return "ripgrep (rg) is not installed or not on PATH. Use the bash tool with grep or find instead." | plan 019 decision 3: no download code |
| rg runs with opencode's environment and no time limit           | rg runs as bash's command does: its own session, the environment bash gives a command (craze's provider keys removed), killed with its group on a cancel, and SIGKILLed at once when the session closes; after 20 s it is stopped with "<grep or glob> tool terminated ripgrep after exceeding timeout 20000 ms. Consider using a more specific path or pattern." (class timeout) | a search must not hold a turn, or outlive it (plan 019 section 3.9) |
| grep shows a match's line with rg's line terminator, so every "  Line n:" line is followed by a blank line, and a CRLF line keeps its "\r" | the terminator ("\n" or "\r\n") is dropped                                                                                   | the blank lines only cost tokens |
| grep cuts a line at 2000 UTF-16 units, then "..."               | at 2000 runes (Unicode code points), never inside a character                                                                                       | Go semantics, as for read |
| a line or path that is not valid UTF-8, which rg reports as base64 bytes, fails the whole grep: "Invalid ripgrep match output" | the bytes are decoded: the line shows U+FFFD for each invalid byte, the path keeps its bytes                                      | one Latin-1 file must not break every search that reaches it |
| a line is shown as rg printed it                                | craze's provider keys are redacted from it before the 2000-rune cut                                                                                 | a key the cut would halve must not leave its first half behind (plan 019 section 3.8) |
| exactly 100 matches (or files) are reported as truncated: "(more matches available)" and the truncation note | only when rg printed a 101st                                                                                                          | exactly 100 is all of them; the note would send the model to narrow a complete answer |
| a grep path that is a file searches the whole directory holding it (grep.ts runs rg in that directory and does not pass the file to Ripgrep.grep) | that file alone, searched by name from its directory; include does not filter it, as rg filters no file it is given by name | grep.test.ts's "supports exact file paths" intends the file; a sibling's matches would mislead |
| a grep path that does not exist searches its parent directory; a glob path that does not exist fails to spawn: "ripgrep execution failed" | refused before rg runs: "grep path does not exist: <path>" / "glob path does not exist: <path>" (not_found)            | a clear message; the parent's matches would mislead |
| glob refuses a file with "glob path must be a directory: <path>"; grep reads whatever the path names | glob refuses anything that is not a directory with those words; grep refuses a path that is neither a file nor a directory: "Path is not a regular file or a directory: <path>" | a FIFO named outright would be read until a writer came (plan 019 section 3.9) |
| a glob rg cannot parse (glob's pattern, grep's include) exits 2 with "error parsing glob", which opencode reads as a search with no rows: "No files found" | rg's message, as an error, as opencode already does for a regex rg cannot parse                         | "No files found" would tell the model the files do not exist |
| an empty grep pattern fails when the call runs: "pattern is required" | refused before it runs, as invalid arguments: "... invalid arguments: pattern is required. ..."                                            | the input is checked before the gate |
| rg failing to start: "ripgrep execution failed", the cause kept out of the message | "ripgrep execution failed: <cause>"                                                                                                       | the model and the transcript see why |
| "\" in a path rg prints becomes "/"                             | kept                                                                                                                                                | a Windows rule; on Linux and macOS "\" is part of a name |
| glob reads `rg --files` a line at a time, so a name with a newline in it splits into two paths, and a file in a directory named "x\n.." reads as "../secret", joined onto the search's parent: a path outside the search, never searched | glob passes --null and splits rg's output on NUL, the one byte a name cannot hold. Every path rg prints, glob's and grep's (whose JSON escapes a newline and so could not split), must lie strictly under the directory searched once cleaned and joined, or the search fails: "Invalid ripgrep output: "<path>" is not a path under <dir>" | a path above the search must never be shown as found in it; rg never prints one, so one means its output cannot be trusted, and failing says so where dropping it would hide it |
| each path, or each file's header, is shown as it is, one a line | a path holding a control character (a newline, a tab, a carriage return) is shown Go-quoted: "/w/x\n../secret"                                  | otherwise a newline in a name reads as two paths, one of them outside the search |
| grep's card title is the pattern; glob's is the directory searched, relative to the worktree | both: the pattern, then " in <path>" when the call names a path, relative to the workspace                                           | one line that says what was searched for, and where; not model-facing |


Tools ported from grok-build
----------------------------

Three tools in this profile are not opencode's. todo_write, ask_user_question
and exit_plan_mode take their ids, their parameter names, their model-facing
