Edits to those descriptions:

  - each opens with one sentence of craze's own naming the tool as Claude Code
    does ("Claude Code calls this tool `TodoWrite`."), so content written for
    Claude Code finds it (D-53);
  - todo_write keeps the template's first two paragraphs;
  - ask_user_question drops the sentence promising an automatic "Other"
    choice: craze's card offers the options and nothing else;
  - exit_plan_mode says "Present your plan to the user for approval" where
    grok-build says "Exit plan mode and present your plan to the user", and
    gains a sentence on what approval and refusal do: here the tool does not
    leave plan mode — approving ends the turn, and the mode is the user's to
    change (D-51) — so grok-build's words would tell the model something false.

Result texts taken from grok-build: a question nobody answered
(ask_user_question/format.rs:22-34) and a plan the person wants revised
(the no-feedback sentence of xai-grok-shell's tool_calls.rs:317-326). A
question's answer is opencode's sentence (question.ts:33-46).

Deliberate differences in behaviour:

| grok-build                                                        | craze                                                                                          | why |
|-------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----|
| approving a plan leaves plan mode and the turn goes on to implement it ("Implement the plan in plan.md.") | approving ends the turn, the mode is unchanged, and the result says so; craze's own "implement" offer follows | D-51 |
| a revised plan can carry the person's feedback; a plan can be abandoned | reject carries none, and there is no abandon: the feedback is the person's next message | craze's ask registry answers a plan with accept or reject |
| an empty plan file exits plan mode: "Plan mode exit approved. No plan content was found" | nobody is asked and nothing changes: the result names the plan file and says to write the plan first | the mode is the person's to change (D-49) |
| a question times out after 30 minutes                             | no timeout                                                                                     | D-52 |
| every question gets a free-text "Other" choice; an option may carry a preview | options only, no preview; Claude Code's optional header is accepted                  | what craze's card shows |
| any number of questions and options                               | at most 8 questions of at most 12 options, and 64 KiB of arguments; an option needs a label and a question at least one option | the card is one screen, and an options-only question with none cannot be answered |
| todo_write returns no id with an item                             | each item carries its id                                                                       | the model needs it to patch a row |
| the list and an id are unbounded                                  | 64 items of 200 bytes, a 64-byte id, 256 KiB and 256 updates a call                            | the list rides in every event |
| exit_plan_mode requires enter_plan_mode                           | there is no enter_plan_mode: the person enters plan mode                                       | plan 023 section 4 |


A tool of craze's own
---------------------

The agent tool (agent.go and descriptions/agent.txt; plan 026 section 3.3)
is neither opencode's nor grok-build's, and its description is craze's own
wording. It opens with one sentence naming the tool as Claude Code and
grok-build do ("Claude Code calls this tool `Agent` (formerly `Task`); grok
calls it `task`."), the pattern of the three tools above (D-53). Its id is
agent where opencode's and grok-build's are task, because in craze's TUI
"tasks" are the todo list. Its first three parameters -- description,
prompt and subagent_type -- are the names opencode's task tool and Claude
Code's Agent tool share; model and effort are craze's own, a choice per
call as codex makes it. Each session appends the agent types and models it
offers to the description (the harness's decorator); the text here is what
testdata/specs.golden pins once the profile registers the tool. Its
run_in_background parameter is Claude Code's name for the same request (plan
026 section 3.11).

The agent_output tool (agent_output.go and descriptions/agent_output.txt;
plan 026 section 3.11), which reads a background agent call's result by the
sub-agent's id, is craze's own as well, in name and wording; its wait_ms is
codex's wait_agent shape (a default of 30 s, at most 600 s).

opencode's truncation hint for an agent that has its task tool
(truncate.ts, "Use the Task tool to have explore agent process this file")
is not ported: a truncated result never invites a sub-agent just to read
it.
